<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2000</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu are a group of nine sites on Okinawa inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2000. They include the castles of Shuri, Nakijin, Zakimi, Katsuren and Nakagusuku, the royal mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaudun">Tamaudun</a>, the Sonohyan-utaki stone gate, the Shikinaen garden and the sacred grove of Sefa-utaki, reflecting Ryukyuan culture from the twelfth to the seventeenth centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gusuku-sites-and-related-properties-of-the-kingdom-of-ryukyu.jpg" alt="Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naha_Shuri_Castle16s5s3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
